{"source_key":"sha256-bb90e64262274e867b24503ac18d6d2f","record_index":113,"record_hash":"sha256:a1c2ce57c3303998a5b440badf224701adfbd9c4836b1f736d89c89000be7e06","timestamp":"2026-09-07T11:58:44.668Z","kind":"message","source_event_kind":"codex:response_item:message","role":"assistant","text":"Yes—the current traversal is doing more than the difference you described. I found two separate problems.\n\n1. **Worktrees force the graph to search for a connection to `HEAD`.** [Worktree tips become ordinary reachable tips]([REDACTED:path]), which receive an entrypoint reachability goal. [Unresolved goals bypass the soft traversal limit]([REDACTED:path]) and keep the entrypoint walking too. An old fork can therefore drag traversal below the normal cutoff; unrelated history can keep it searching to the roots. Filtering afterward cannot recover that cost.\n\n2. **The listing excludes displayed stack commits, rather than `HEAD` ancestry.** [The projection]([REDACTED:path]) builds its exclusion set from workspace stacks, then stops at those commits or target markers. That misses commits absent from the projection—for example, the synthetic workspace merge itself.\n\nYour proposed model is the right starting point:\n\n```text\neligible = ancestors(worktree_head) − ancestors(HEAD)\n```\n\nHere, ancestry includes the starting commit. If the cutoff is reachable from `HEAD`, this already excludes the cutoff and everything beneath it. If the cutoff can lie outside `HEAD` ancestry, its ancestry needs an explicit additional exclusion.\n\nThere are two existing presentation choices to keep separate from that rule: the listing currently follows first parents, and earlier worktrees claim shared commits. Literal revset output follows all parents and can overlap between worktrees.\n\nI’d resolve it in this order:\n\n1. **Add regression fixtures before changing traversal.** Cover worktrees exactly at and immediately above workspace `HEAD`, an old fork with a small unique delta, a merge with an excluded parent, and unrelated history. Assert commit membership against `git rev-list worktree_head ^HEAD`, accounting separately for display ownership. Measure traversal work as well as output.\n\n2. **Distinguish worktree seeds within the existing graph walker.** Preserve their identity so they do not inherit the ordinary “keep searching until connected” policy. Apply explicit ancestry exclusions and bounded traversal without allowing worktree goals to extend the main workspace’s traversal budget. Keep this in the existing queue and classification machinery.\n\n3. **Project from that graph classification.** Replace the displayed-stack exclusion heuristic with graph-derived eligibility. Preserve worktree ownership and base placement as presentation decisions, with explicit merge coverage.\n\n4. **Validate shared consumers.** Run graph and workspace tests, then CLI status/listing and TUI regressions. The bare worktree enumeration API does not walk commit history, but [`but worktree list` constructs an `IdMap`]([REDACTED:path]), which brings graph construction into that path.\n\nExact ancestry queries may still require history inspection; a small result alone does not guarantee cheap traversal. The regression should demonstrate that worktree listing no longer unnecessarily expands workspace traversal.\n\nNo files changed. I attempted the focused workspace tests, but Cargo failed before compilation because `crates/gitbutler-stack/Cargo.toml` is missing. These findings are source-derived; the failing regression remains the first implementation step.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:197-216|note=[located prior worktree listing boundary and verified current callers]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<rollout_ids>\n[REDACTED:entropy]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<[REDACTED:path]>","tool_name":null,"tool_input":null,"source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-09-07T11:58:44.668Z","ordinal":113,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","role":"as